The following benchmarks have been taken from the Kleenex repository
(https://github.com/diku-kmc/kleenexlang) and are licensed under the MIT
license (together with the data generators):

- apache_log
- datetime_to_json
- email

Benchmarks for --vectorize-loops (vec_*)
----------------------------------------

See REPRODUCIBLE.md for the fixed-length, periodic-escape, end-of-buffer and
repository-source regimes, their oracle checks, and short reproduction commands
for both google/benchmark and Go testing.B.

The vec_* benchmarks compare scalar and vectorized lexers generated from the
same rules (src/re2c/vec_strings_rules.re, included by vec_00__strings.re;
driver in src/re2c/vec_base.re). The variants differ only in the input model
and in re2c:vectorize:loops:

- sentinel: whole file in memory, NUL sentinel, no bounds checks (fastest
  scalar lexer; it cannot be vectorized, as vectorization needs bounds checks)
- bounded-scalar / bounded-vector: whole file in memory with bounds checks
- buffered-scalar / buffered-vector: fixed-size buffer refilled with YYFILL

- bounded-vector-fixed / buffered-vector-fixed: the same as bounded-vector /
  buffered-vector, but generated from vec_01__strings_fixed.re with
  `re2c --syntax src/re2c/vec_fixed.syntax`, which overrides code:vector_loop
  with a vector loop that tests the class itself (one range check per range of
  the class, `range` list) and moves the cursor to the first byte that leaves
  the loop by counting the trailing zeros of the inverted mask. The default
  template tests the complement of the class instead (one equality comparison
  per byte that leaves the loop, `xrange` list); both advance the cursor to the
  exit byte

Reference implementations that do not come from re2c (src/ref/):

- ref-libc: hand-written lexer for the same rules that skips string and
  comment bodies with glibc strcspn/strstr/strspn (which have SSE2/SSE4.2/AVX2
  versions selected at run time), sentinel input model
- ref-switch: plain hand-written byte-at-a-time switch lexer, sentinel input
  model

In-memory benchmarks read the file once outside of the timed loop; buffered
benchmarks include reading the file in chunks. The test binary checks that all
variants, including ref-libc and ref-switch, produce identical token streams (a
digest of all rule/length pairs).

Optional third-party references (off by default, not vendored, found with
pkg-config). They perform DIFFERENT tasks and do not produce the same token
stream, so they are only an indication of what SIMD code can achieve on the
same bytes:

- -DRE2C_BENCH_SIMDJSON=ON (simdjson >= 3, C++17): on the json data set only
  (a sequence of 256 JSON documents), simdjson-dom is a full DOM parse
  (parse_many), simdjson-ondemand is an On-Demand parse that visits every value
  and unescapes every string (iterate_many), simdjson-stage1 is only the SIMD
  structural indexing (stage 1) of the whole file. JSON validation and parsing
  is a different task from tokenization with the rules above. Threaded
  document streams are disabled.
- -DRE2C_BENCH_HYPERSCAN=ON (Hyperscan or Vectorscan, pkg-config name libhs):
  hyperscan-block scans each data set in block mode with the seven token
  regular expressions (all rules except the default one) and counts matches.
  Hyperscan reports the end offset of every match of every pattern (every
  prefix of an identifier matches, the default rule does not exist), so it
  neither selects the longest match nor restarts after a token; the number of
  matches (reported as a counter) is not the number of tokens.

On Debian/Ubuntu they are available as packages (libsimdjson-dev,
libhyperscan-dev); otherwise install them into a prefix and add
$PREFIX/lib/pkgconfig to PKG_CONFIG_PATH. For example (not vendored in the
repository, use any directory outside of it):

    git clone --depth 1 --branch v3.6.4 https://github.com/simdjson/simdjson \
        /path/to/simdjson-src
    cmake -S /path/to/simdjson-src -B /path/to/simdjson-build \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/refs-prefix
    cmake --build /path/to/simdjson-build --target install

Vectorscan (https://github.com/VectorCamp/vectorscan) builds the same way
(it needs Boost headers, Ragel 6 and Python 3).

Data sets (benchmarks/_data/{strshort,strmedium,strlong,strescape,json,csource},
regenerated deterministically by benchmarks/_data/gen_strings.py): string
literals of 0-24 bytes (below the 33-byte guard of the vector loop), 32-128
bytes, 256-4096 bytes, 32-256 bytes with ~15% escape sequences, pretty-printed
JSON-like records, and C++ source code from re2c 4.6.

The generated code uses AVX2 intrinsics, so vec_* objects (and only them) are
compiled with -mavx2. They are built only on x86; the benchmark and test
binaries skip them at run time if the CPU does not support AVX2.

To reproduce (google/benchmark installed in $GBENCH_PREFIX; drop the two
RE2C_BENCH_* options to build without the third-party references):

    PKG_CONFIG_PATH=$GBENCH_PREFIX/lib/pkgconfig cmake -S . -B .build-bench \
        -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DRE2C_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON \
        -DRE2C_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \
        -DRE2C_BENCH_SIMDJSON=ON -DRE2C_BENCH_HYPERSCAN=ON
    cmake --build .build-bench --target bench_submatch_aot
    cd .build-bench/benchmarks/c
    ./test
    taskset -c 8 ./bench-gcc --benchmark_filter='^vec-' \
        --benchmark_repetitions=5 --benchmark_report_aggregates_only=true
    taskset -c 8 ./bench-clang --benchmark_filter='^vec-' \
        --benchmark_repetitions=5 --benchmark_report_aggregates_only=true

To measure cycles per byte of one benchmark, run it twice with a fixed number
of iterations and divide the difference of cycles by the difference of bytes
(this cancels the start-up cost, e.g. reading the input file):

    for n in 20 220; do
        taskset -c 8 perf stat -x, -e cycles:u,instructions:u ./bench-gcc \
            --benchmark_filter='^vec-json-gcc_re2c-bounded-vector-fixed$' \
            --benchmark_min_time=${n}x 2>&1 >/dev/null | grep cycles
    done
    # cycles/byte = (cycles(220) - cycles(20)) / (200 * size of data/json/big)

(on hybrid Intel CPUs, use cpu_core/cycles/u and cpu_core/instructions/u).
